% approval_quorum — decide when a room's proposal may become a pull request

# Module: approval_quorum

## Intent

The Ensemble agent has read-only access to the codebase. It can propose a
patch, but it can never write to the repository. A proposal becomes a real
pull request only when the room agrees.

This module is the gate. It answers one question: given who is in the room and
how they voted, may this proposal be opened as a PR, and if not, what is it
waiting on?

This is pure decision logic. No I/O, no network, no clock, no global state.

## Interface

```python
def evaluate(
    members: list[str],
    votes: list[dict],
    policy: str = "unanimous",
) -> dict:
    ...
```

- `members` — every participant currently in the room, by user_id.
- `votes` — append-only vote log, oldest first. Each entry is
  `{"user_id": str, "verdict": "approve" | "request_changes"}`.
  A participant may vote more than once; only their LAST vote counts.
- `policy` — one of:
  - `"unanimous"` — every member must approve
  - `"majority"` — more than half of members must approve
- Returns:
  ```python
  {
      "can_open_pr": bool,
      "approved_by": list[str],   # sorted, user_ids whose last vote was approve
      "blocked_by": list[str],    # sorted, user_ids whose last vote was request_changes
      "waiting_on": list[str],    # sorted, members who have not voted at all
      "reason": str,              # short human-readable explanation
  }
  ```

## MUST

- MUST count only each participant's most recent vote.
- MUST return `can_open_pr` as `False` whenever `blocked_by` is non-empty,
  under every policy. A single `request_changes` is a veto.
- MUST return `can_open_pr` as `False` when `members` is empty.
- MUST ignore votes from user_ids that are not in `members`, and MUST NOT let
  them appear in any of the three returned lists.
- MUST return all three lists sorted ascending, so two clients rendering the
  same state show the same order.
- MUST make `approved_by`, `blocked_by`, and `waiting_on` mutually exclusive,
  and together they MUST cover exactly `members`.
- MUST raise `ValueError` for an unknown `policy`.
- MUST raise `ValueError` for a vote whose `verdict` is neither `"approve"`
  nor `"request_changes"`.

## MUST NOT

- MUST NOT mutate `members` or `votes`.
- MUST NOT treat a missing vote as an approval.
- MUST NOT let a duplicate member in `members` be counted twice.
- MUST NOT perform any I/O.

## Examples

```python
evaluate(["amy", "joe"], [{"user_id": "amy", "verdict": "approve"}])
# -> can_open_pr False, approved_by ["amy"], waiting_on ["joe"],
#    reason "waiting on joe"

evaluate(["amy", "joe"], [{"user_id": "amy", "verdict": "approve"},
                          {"user_id": "joe", "verdict": "approve"}])
# -> can_open_pr True, reason "2 of 2 approved"

# Last vote wins: joe changed his mind, so the veto is lifted.
evaluate(["amy", "joe"], [{"user_id": "amy", "verdict": "approve"},
                          {"user_id": "joe", "verdict": "request_changes"},
                          {"user_id": "joe", "verdict": "approve"}])
# -> can_open_pr True

# A veto outranks a majority.
evaluate(["a", "b", "c"], [{"user_id": "a", "verdict": "approve"},
                           {"user_id": "b", "verdict": "approve"},
                           {"user_id": "c", "verdict": "request_changes"}],
         policy="majority")
# -> can_open_pr False, blocked_by ["c"]

evaluate([], [])
# -> can_open_pr False, reason "no members in room"
```

## Done when

- Every example above returns exactly the stated result.
- The module has no imports beyond the standard library.
